    <http://example.org/node/chunk1> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocols and applicable regulation; assistance in drafting medical and administrative documents; support in handling dossiers (admissions, coding, medico-social pathways, etc.); support for the continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. The solution must fit into the existing practices of staff and must not introduce a rupture in clinical or administrative workflows. 3. Functional scope 3.1 Information consultation assistance. The solution must let staff quickly access reliable, up-to-date information drawn from the authority's internal procedures, care protocols and HAS recommendations, applicable regulatory texts, and internal documents (guides, notes, reference frameworks, drug references). The solution must contextualise The solution answers to the staff member's request. 3.2 Drafting assistance. The solution must assist the drafting of clinical and administrative content, notably discharge summaries and medical reports (comptes-rendus), medical correspondence and patient letters, administrative documents and dossier syntheses. Generated content must be adapted to the clinical/administrative context and conform to expected quality standards. 3.3 Dossier-handling assistance. The solution must assist staff in analysing and handling dossiers by surfacing relevant information, recalling applicable rules, and suggesting actions or verifications. The solution must support medical coding (PMSI / ICD-10) for activity reporting (T2A)." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk10> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "This governance structure is essential for maintaining decision traceability and ensuring that all agent contributions are consolidated into a coherent bid response. our company's dedicated team, comprising a project director, solution architect, engineers, data scientist, and security compliance lead, will work closely with the consortium partners to gather inputs and develop a comprehensive bid response. our company's will utilize our company's adapted version of standard public-sector project frameworks, with documented deliverables at each gate, to guide the consortium efforts. To ensure that all requirements are addressed, our company's will conduct a thorough analysis of the client's needs, using our company's expertise in delivering sovereign AI platforms, such as our company's flagship product Atelier. our flagship product Atelier has been successfully deployed in various public sector and health projects, demonstrating our flagship product Atelier ability to forge document-grounded AI assistants from an organization's own documents. By following our company's phased approach, which includes Framing, Design, Pilot, Generalisation, and Run phases, each ending with a formal review and a go/no-go decision, our company's will be able to progressively develop and refine our company's bid response." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk11> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "This will enable our company's to provide a robust and tailored solution that meets the client's requirements while maintaining transparency and traceability throughout the process. Ultimately, our company's goal is to submit a coherent and compelling bid response that showcases the consortium capabilities and demonstrates our company's commitment to delivering a high-quality solution that addresses the client's needs and constraints." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk12> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires a solution with response times below 2 seconds for common queries, high availability (including outside office hours), API integration with existing information systems and user directories, hosting on sovereign infrastructure certified to the level required by the tender within the EU, and no dependency on non-European suppliers. For each requirement, state whether each requirement is FULLY COVERED, PARTIALLY COVERED or NOT COVERED by our current technical stack, and describe the proposed architecture. The client requirements can be addressed as follows: 1. Response times below 2 seconds for common queries: FULLY COVERED. our platform's performance metrics indicate that common single-document queries are below 2 seconds at p95 latency. 2. High availability (including outside office hours): FULLY COVERED. our offer 24/7 operation with an availability target of 99.9% on the appropriate hosting tier, ensuring high availability including outside office hours. 3." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk13> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "API integration with existing information systems: FULLY COVERED. our architecture and integration capabilities include API-first design (REST + webhook) that can be embedded in existing work environments, facilitating integration with existing hospital information systems. 4. API integration with user directories: FULLY COVERED. our support single sign-on (SSO) protocols such as SAML and OIDC, as well as user directories like LDAP and Entra ID, ensuring seamless integration with client's user directories. 5. Hosting on sovereign infrastructure certified to the level required by the tender within the EU: FULLY COVERED. our hosting options include the Sovereign HDS tier provided by OuraneCloud, which is SecNumCloud-qualified, HDS-certified, and located within the EU, meeting the client's sovereignty requirements. 6. No dependency on non-European suppliers: FULLY COVERED. Given our model options include a sovereign European open-weight model hosted by Faber IA on OuraneCloud, our can ensure that our solution does not depend on non-European suppliers for critical components, aligning with the client's requirement for data sovereignty and reduced external dependency." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk14> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The proposed architecture would leverage our Atelier platform, utilizing our Atelier platform retrieval-augmented generation capabilities to provide efficient and accurate responses to queries. The proposed architecture would be hosted on OuraneCloud Sovereign HDS tier to ensure compliance with the EU data sovereignty regulations. Integration with existing information systems and user directories would be facilitated through our API-first approach and support for standard SSO protocols and user directory services. By leveraging Our platform's capabilities and hosting infrastructure, our can meet The client requirements for response time, availability, integration, sovereignty, and supplier dependency." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk15> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The tender requires strict GDPR compliance (EU-only data hosting, no transfer outside EU, encryption and pseudonymisation of sensitive data where applicable), explainability and traceability of AI outputs, and adherence to French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. What security risks should we flag, and are we compliant with these requirements? Based on we company context, I can confirm that we have experience delivering solutions compliant with GDPR for public-sector and health clients, including special-category health data. we host we data exclusively within EU-only through we hosting partner OuraneCloud, which holds SecNumCloud qualification, ensuring data sovereignty." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk16> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Regarding security risks, potential flags include the need to ensure strict access controls, secure data transmission via TLS 1.3, and encryption at rest using AES-256. Additionally, we must maintain segregation of duties between development and production operations and adhere to defined remediation SLAs by severity for vulnerability management. we are compliant with GDPR requirements as we have been ISO 27001 certified since 2023, with a scope covering development, delivery, and operations. we ISO 27701 certification also ensures privacy information management. Furthermore, we hosting infrastructure operated on our hosting partner OuraneCloud, which holds SecNumCloud qualification holds HDS certification, which covers Faber IA's processing on our hosting infrastructure operated on OuraneCloud." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk17> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "For explainability and traceability of AI outputs, we product Atelier supports these features. our product Atelier is grounded in sources, allows retrieval trace, has an audit log, and supports a posteriori review and medical confidentiality. However, I do not have information on whether we current solution fully meets the specific requirements of French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification beyond hosting infrastructure compliance. But we can provide audit evidence linking platform features to applicable obligations." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk18> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "In conclusion, while there are potential security risks that need to be addressed, such as access controls and secure data transmission, we is generally compliant with the specified requirements, including GDPR, data sovereignty, and explainability of AI outputs." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk19> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The tender requires compliance with regulatory requirements, including GDPR, medical confidentiality, and non-automated clinical or medico-administrative decisions without explicit validation by a qualified professional. What legal risks should we flag, and are we compliant with regulatory requirements, including GDPR, medical confidentiality, and non-automated clinical or medico-administrative decisions without explicit validation by a qualified professional? Based on we company context, we have experience with French public procurement and compliance with regulatory requirements such as GDPR, medical confidentiality, and the HDS framework. we Security & Compliance function monitors these obligations, and we have administrative attestations available to provide the standard attestations required for French public procurement." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message9> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk2> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The solution must take the dossier context into account where available. No decision may be automated. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of care. The solution must facilitate the continuity of care between professionals and services by enabling relevant information sharing, an understanding of the context of an in-progress patient pathway, and an efficient resumption of handling by another professional. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints 4.1 Personal and health data protection. The titleholder must guarantee strict compliance with the GDPR. Health data is processed as special-category data (GDPR Art. 9) and medical confidentiality (secret médical) must be preserved. Data must be hosted exclusively within the European Union; no transfer of data outside the European Union is authorised; sensitive data must be appropriately protected (encryption, pseudonymisation where relevant). 4.2 Health-data hosting (HDS). The infrastructure hosting The solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. 4.3 Explainability and traceability. Recommendations or suggestions produced by The solution must be explainable. It must be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements that led to an output (source grounding, audit trail), consistent with HAS guidance on the use of AI in healthcare. 4.4 Medical responsibility. The solution must be designed as an assistance tool. No clinical or medico-administrative decision may be automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR); should any use case approach medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), the bidder must explicitly address the implications. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk20> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Regarding GDPR, we ensure strict compliance, including Art. 9 special-category data and Art. 28 processing agreements. we also respect medical confidentiality requirements. Additionally, we approach to AI models includes using a sovereign European open-weight model under a sovereign European open-weight model open-weight license and accessing proprietary third-party models under commercial licenses with documented terms and supplier dependencies. However, we should flag the risk of non-compliance if we solution were to introduce automated clinical or medico-administrative decisions without explicit validation by a qualified professional. our company context does not explicitly address the risk of non-compliance, but we do have a Legal function that flags CE/MDR implications and liability consequences when use cases approach the medical-device threshold." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message9> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk21> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "To mitigate potential risks, we should ensure our solution is designed as an assistance tool, not requiring medical device qualification or CE marking under the MDR. we also need to guarantee that any recommendations or suggestions produced by our solution are explainable, with an audit trail allowing for understanding of the elements leading to the output. Overall, while we have a foundation for compliance with regulatory requirements, we must carefully review and address the specific constraints related to automated decisions and ensure our solution aligns with the specific constraints related to automated decisions." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message9> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk22> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client's global budget is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years, with an annual operating cost that must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. Given our pricing model and current financial position, what is our projected margin on this contract, and are there cost-optimisation strategies our can propose? Based on the provided context, our standard daily rates range from 700 to 1300 per day, excluding VAT." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message11> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk23> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The target gross margin percentage is between 25% and 35%. To estimate our projected margin on this contract, our need to consider the budget envelope, which includes one-off project costs and recurring run costs. one-off project costs are estimated at 30% of the reference budget, which is 900,000, resulting in 270,000 over 18 months. recurring run costs are estimated at 70% of the reference budget, which is 630,000 over 5 years. Given our current financial position and pricing model, our can aim to achieve a gross margin within the target range of 25% to 35%." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message11> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk24> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "However, without more specific details on the project scope, resource allocation, and cost structure, it's challenging to provide an exact projected margin. As for cost-optimisation strategies, our can propose the following:  1. **Favouring sovereign HDS tier and European model**: As mentioned in our budget envelope context, Favouring sovereign HDS tier and European model is more cost-efficient on the recurring side compared to the premium high-availability tier combined with top-tier proprietary inference. 2. **Optimising hosting and inference costs**: Since hosting and inference costs are the main recurring cost drivers, our can work with the technical team to select the most cost-effective options while meeting the client's requirements. 3. **Efficient resource allocation**: By carefully planning and allocating our resources, such as project directors, solution architects, data scientists, and engineers, our can minimize costs and maximize value for the client. By adopting these strategies, our can work within the client's budgetary constraints while aiming to achieve a gross margin within our target range." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message11> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk25> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires a solution that provides intelligent assistance to hospital staff, facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times. How can our AI capabilities be leveraged to meet these requirements, and what innovation strategies can our propose to enhance the solution? Our company, Faber IA, offers AI-powered solutions that can be leveraged to meet the client's requirements. our retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) approach allows our to provide intelligent assistance to hospital staff by retrieving relevant passages from hospital staff controlled corpus and generating answers grounded in hospital staff own validated documents." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk26> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "This capability facilitates access to information, as required by the client. To improve the quality of written outputs, our template-guided drafting capability can assist in drafting clinical and administrative content, such as discharge summaries and medical correspondence. Additionally, our structured extraction capability can help with tasks like PMSI/ICD-10 coding, ensuring accuracy and consistency in written outputs." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk27> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Regarding reducing processing and documentation times, our solution can surface information and draft content at the point of need, streamlining clinical and administrative workflows. our design principle is to embed the assistant into the staff's existing work environment and tools, avoiding disruption and ensuring seamless integration. To enhance our solution, our propose an iterative approach, starting with corpus selection and curation, followed by retrieval tuning, prompt and template design, and evaluation loops. an iterative approach, starting with corpus selection and curation, followed by retrieval tuning, prompt and template design, and evaluation loops ensures that our solution is tailored to the client's specific needs and workflows. In terms of innovation strategies, our can propose leveraging our Sovereign European open-weight model, which offers fully auditable and explainable AI capabilities, aligning with the client's requirements for data sovereignty and transparency. our can also explore optional fine-tuning on client material, while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and data protection regulations." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk28> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "By combining our AI capabilities with an iterative and collaborative approach, our can develop a tailored solution that meets the client's needs and enhances the client's clinical and administrative workflows." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk29> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client wishes to limit the environmental footprint of The client information systems, with a focus on reducing energy consumption and relying on low-carbon infrastructure. What sustainability strategies can we propose to reduce the environmental impact of we solution, and how can we demonstrate we commitment to CSR and sustainability? we can propose several sustainability strategies to reduce the environmental impact of our solution. Firstly, we can leverage we green hosting partner, OuraneCloud, which uses renewable energy and has a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2, indicating efficient energy use." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message15> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk3> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "As an indication, a response time below 2 seconds is expected for common queries. A high level of answer relevance is expected. 5.2 Availability. Continuity of care requires a high level of availability, including outside office hours (24/7 services). An availability target of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration and interoperability. The solution must integrate with the existing hospital information system (SIH) and the electronic patient record (DPI). The solution must support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). API-type interfaces are expected, as is integration with user directories. 6. Sovereignty constraints the authority's attaches particular importance to data sovereignty. Hosting must be performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure or equivalent, and particular attention will be paid to dependency on non-European suppliers. 7. Budget constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years. The annual operating cost must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. The titleholder must propose an approach that optimises costs while guaranteeing the expected quality of service. 8. Environmental constraints As part of the authority's sustainable-development policy, the authority's wishes to limit the environmental footprint of the authority's information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. 9. Deployment terms the project must be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter (one or two departments) followed by progressive generalisation to all services." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk30> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Additionally, OuraneCloud reuses heat, further reducing waste and environmental impact. we also practice digital sobriety through model right-sizing, caching, and batching, which helps minimize the computational resources required and subsequently reduces energy consumption. Furthermore, we carbon reporting capability allows we to monitor and report on the carbon footprint of our solution, enabling we to identify areas for improvement and optimize we sustainability performance. To demonstrate our commitment to CSR and sustainability, we can highlight we published trajectory to reduce carbon intensity per managed assistant year on year, showcasing we proactive approach to minimizing our environmental footprint. we Ecovadis Silver rating and adherence to a robust CSR governance structure, where a dedicated CSR lead reports to the executive team, underscore we dedication to sustainability and social responsibility." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message15> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk31> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "By emphasizing our commitment to CSR and sustainability, we can assure the client that our solution not only meets the client technical requirements but also aligns with the client values of reducing energy consumption and relying on low-carbon infrastructure, thereby supporting the client environmental sustainability goals." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message15> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk32> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) can leverage our consortium's expertise in delivering sovereign AI platforms to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and enhance patient care. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) proposed solution, built on The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) flagship product Atelier, utilizes retrieval-augmented generation capabilities to provide efficient and accurate responses to queries, facilitating access to information and improving the quality of written outputs. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) hybrid delivery model, combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure, will guide the project's progression, ensuring that all stakeholders are aligned and informed throughout. A joint steering committee and operational committee will be established to maintain decision traceability and consolidate agent contributions into a coherent bid response. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) dedicated team, comprising a project director, solution architect, engineers, data scientist, and security compliance lead, will work closely with consortium partners to gather inputs and develop a comprehensive bid response. Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, leverages The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) our flagship product Atelier platform, hosted on OuraneCloud's Sovereign HDS tier, ensuring compliance with EU data sovereignty regulations. This infrastructure is SecNumCloud-qualified, HDS-certified, and located within EU, meeting the client's sovereignty requirements. Integration with existing information systems and user directories will be facilitated through The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) API-first approach and support for standard SSO protocols and user directory services. Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, provides response times below 2 seconds for common queries, meeting the client's performance expectations." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk33> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "High availability, including outside office hours, is ensured through 24/7 operation with an availability target of 99.9% on the appropriate hosting tier. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) also support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). Regarding security and compliance, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) have experience delivering solutions compliant with GDPR for public-sector and health clients, including special-category health data. OuraneCloud's ensures data sovereignty, and The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) maintain segregation of duties between development and production operations. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) are ISO 27001 certified, with a scope covering development, delivery, and operations, and The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) hosting infrastructure operated on OuraneCloud's holds HDS certification. Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, is designed as an assistance tool, not requiring medical device qualification or CE marking under the MDR. Recommendations or suggestions produced by Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, are explainable, with an audit trail allowing for understanding of the elements leading to the output. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) guarantee strict compliance with GDPR, including Art. 9 special-category data and Art. 28 processing agreements, and respect medical confidentiality requirements. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) pricing model is based on standard daily rates ranging from €700 to €1300 per day, excluding VAT, with a target gross margin percentage between 25% and 35%." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk34> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) estimate one-off project costs at 30% of the reference budget (€270,000 over 18 months) and recurring run costs at 70% of the reference budget (€630,000 over 5 years). To optimize costs, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) propose favouring sovereign HDS tier and European model, optimizing hosting and inference costs, and efficient resource allocation. Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, leverages retrieval-augmented generation to provide intelligent assistance to hospital staff. Template-guided drafting capability assists in drafting clinical and administrative content, while structured extraction capability helps with tasks like PMSI/ICD-10 coding. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) design principle is to embed the assistant into the staff's existing work environment and tools, avoiding disruption and ensuring seamless integration. To reduce environmental impact, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) leverage green hosting partner OuraneCloud, which uses renewable energy and has a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) practice digital sobriety through model right-sizing, caching, and batching, minimizing computational resources required and reducing energy consumption. The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) carbon reporting capability monitors and reports on the carbon footprint of Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier,, enabling optimization of sustainability performance. Our proposed solution, built on our flagship product Atelier, aligns with the client's requirements, ensuring data sovereignty, security, and compliance while providing a tailored and innovative AI-powered assistant to enhance clinical and administrative workflows." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk35> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) are committed to delivering a high-quality solution that meets the client's needs and constraints, while minimizing environmental impact and supporting the client's sustainability goals." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk4> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Change-management support and staff training are expected. 10. Evaluation criteria Offers will be evaluated against the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution; compliance with regulatory requirements; level of security; data sovereignty; quality of integration with the existing SIH/DPI; total cost of the proposed solution; consideration of environmental issues; capacity for innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk5> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocols and applicable regulation; assistance in drafting medical and administrative documents; support in handling dossiers (admissions, coding, medico-social pathways, etc.); support for the continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. The solution must fit into the existing practices of staff and must not introduce a rupture in clinical or administrative workflows. 3. Functional scope 3.1 Information consultation assistance. The solution must let staff quickly access reliable, up-to-date information drawn from the authority's internal procedures, care protocols and HAS recommendations, applicable regulatory texts, and internal documents (guides, notes, reference frameworks, drug references). The solution must contextualise The solution answers to the staff member's request. 3.2 Drafting assistance. The solution must assist the drafting of clinical and administrative content, notably discharge summaries and medical reports (comptes-rendus), medical correspondence and patient letters, administrative documents and dossier syntheses. Generated content must be adapted to the clinical/administrative context and conform to expected quality standards. 3.3 Dossier-handling assistance. The solution must assist staff in analysing and handling dossiers by surfacing relevant information, recalling applicable rules, and suggesting actions or verifications. The solution must support medical coding (PMSI / ICD-10) for activity reporting (T2A)." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk6> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The solution must take the dossier context into account where available. No decision may be automated. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of care. The solution must facilitate the continuity of care between professionals and services by enabling relevant information sharing, an understanding of the context of an in-progress patient pathway, and an efficient resumption of handling by another professional. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints 4.1 Personal and health data protection. The titleholder must guarantee strict compliance with the GDPR. Health data is processed as special-category data (GDPR Art. 9) and medical confidentiality (secret médical) must be preserved. Data must be hosted exclusively within the European Union; no transfer of data outside the European Union is authorised; sensitive data must be appropriately protected (encryption, pseudonymisation where relevant). 4.2 Health-data hosting (HDS). The infrastructure hosting The solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. 4.3 Explainability and traceability. Recommendations or suggestions produced by The solution must be explainable. It must be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements that led to an output (source grounding, audit trail), consistent with HAS guidance on the use of AI in healthcare. 4.4 Medical responsibility. The solution must be designed as an assistance tool. No clinical or medico-administrative decision may be automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR); should any use case approach medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), the bidder must explicitly address the implications. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk7> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "As an indication, a response time below 2 seconds is expected for common queries. A high level of answer relevance is expected. 5.2 Availability. Continuity of care requires a high level of availability, including outside office hours (24/7 services). An availability target of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration and interoperability. The solution must integrate with the existing hospital information system (SIH) and the electronic patient record (DPI). The solution must support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). API-type interfaces are expected, as is integration with user directories. 6. Sovereignty constraints the authority's attaches particular importance to data sovereignty. Hosting must be performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure or equivalent, and particular attention will be paid to dependency on non-European suppliers. 7. Budget constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years. The annual operating cost must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. The titleholder must propose an approach that optimises costs while guaranteeing the expected quality of service. 8. Environmental constraints As part of the authority's sustainable-development policy, the authority's wishes to limit the environmental footprint of the authority's information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. 9. Deployment terms the project must be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter (one or two departments) followed by progressive generalisation to all services." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk8> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Change-management support and staff training are expected. 10. Evaluation criteria Offers will be evaluated against the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution; compliance with regulatory requirements; level of security; data sovereignty; quality of integration with the existing SIH/DPI; total cost of the proposed solution; consideration of environmental issues; capacity for innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk9> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Given our company's project management methodologies and past public sector references, how can our company's best coordinate the consortium to respond to this tender, ensuring that all agent contributions are consolidated into a coherent bid response while maintaining decision traceability? To coordinate the consortium and respond to this tender, I will leverage our company's hybrid delivery model combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure. a phase-gate structure is suited to public and health procurement and has been successfully applied in our company's past references, such as the regional council and mutual health insurer projects. Firstly, our company's will establish a joint steering committee with the client, meeting monthly, and an operational committee meeting fortnightly, to ensure that all stakeholders are aligned and informed throughout the project." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

